 Reports / Audit Report of the Department of Design and Construction’s Mitigation of Delays and Cost Overruns on Capital Construction Projects 

 ARCHIVE from Comptroller Lander's Administration 

 Audit & Investigation

 Audit Report of the Department of Design and Construction’s Mitigation of Delays and Cost Overruns on Capital Construction Projects

 December 26, 2025 
 | SE23-073A 

 Table of Contents

sections.Scope and Methodology:
We conducted this performance audit in accordance with Generally Accepted Government Auditing Standards (GAGAS). GAGAS requires that we plan and perform the audit to obtain sufficient, appropriate evidence to provide a reasonable basis for our findings and conclusions based on our audit objectives. We believe that the evidence obtained provides a reasonable basis for our findings and conclusions within the context of our audit objective(s). This audit was conducted in accordance with the audit responsibilities of the City Comptroller as set forth in Chapter 5, §93, of the New York City Charter.

The scope of this audit was capital projects with Substantial Completion dates after FY2018, with the status of projects extended to April 2025.

Auditors researched relevant websites and identified and reviewed external reports and prior audits by the New York City Comptroller’s Office to assemble information about DDC and projects that DDC handles on behalf of other City agencies.

To obtain an understanding of DDC’s processes, policies and procedures, internal controls, and risk associated with administration of construction projects, the auditors conducted walkthrough meetings with Project Managers, Program Directors, Associate and Deputy Commissioners, DDC’s General Counsel, and other appropriate personnel familiar with DDC’s policies and procedures and projects covered in this audit. Auditors documented their understanding through memos and flowcharts and obtained DDC’s confirmations.

Auditors obtained and reviewed relevant governing documents used by DDC in administration of the contracts. Also, where applicable, auditors used the following documents as the criteria:

• 

• 

• DDC Construction Project Manual (CPM Manual)

• DDC Design Consultant Guide

• DDC Public Buildings Division Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)

• DDC Infrastructure Division Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)

• DDC Project Controls Guide

• DDC Benchmark Manual

• NYC Standard Construction Contract

To obtain an understanding of DDC’s reporting of its construction management services and process improvement plans, auditors reviewed:

• 

• 

• DDC’s website

• DDC’s reporting in the Mayor’s Management Report

• DDC’s 2019 Strategic Plan and updates

To obtain an understanding of the records associated with the projects administered by DDC, auditors:

• 

• 

• Obtained a list of capital projects with Substantial Completion dates between FY2018 and FY2023

• Examined project information on DDC Anywhere Website to which DDC provided auditors access

• Examined financial and payment information through the Checkbook NYC Website (publicly available system administered by the NYC Office of the Comptroller)

• Examined contract and vendor information through the NYC PASSPort Website (publicly available system administered by the Mayor’s Office of Contract Services)

Auditors obtained a list of projects that were administered by DDC during the scope period and evaluated DDC project data to obtain an understanding of project distribution by client agency, project amounts, overall delays, and delays in design and construction phases.[42] Auditors conducted data analysis of projects categorized as FEP projects.

To assess DDC’s management of high-risk capital projects, auditors selected a sample of three projects from major client agencies—DSNY, DEP, and DOT—based on factors such as project size, delay severity, and significance, ensuring coverage of diverse infrastructure types and agency-specific challenges, to review and perform an in-depth analysis of how effectively DDC mitigated delays and cost overruns.

Auditors assessed the following three sampled projects involving the client agencies—DSNY, DEP, and DOT—in detail:

• 

• 

• Staten Island Garage 1 & 3 Construction and Rehabilitation Project (S136-367, Phase II); Client agency – DSNY

• Installation of 36” Water Main and other Infrastructure along 9th Avenue (MED-609B, 9th Avenue Extension); Client agency – DEP

• Owl’s Head Connector Section of the Brooklyn Waterfront Gateway (HWK1048G); Client agency – DOT

Auditors obtained project records from DDC related to the sampled projects, including, but not limited to:

• 

• 

• Executed Contracts

• Project Specifications

• Project Drawings

• Change Order Logs

• Project Schedules

• Time Extension(s) documentation

• Delay Analysis Logs

• Relevant correspondence between DDC and Project Sponsor Agencies

Auditors reviewed information and documentation in Benchmark and requested additional project management documents from DDC as needed. They also evaluated whether project information was consistent in Benchmark.

Auditors met with the DDC project teams involved in the selected projects and also with representatives from the sponsor agencies.

Auditors also conducted physical observations of a sampled DSNY Garage in Staten Island project, which was in the construction phase, and met with the Construction Manager’s staff.

Auditors followed up with DDC for clarification or additional information when required.

Although the results of the sampling tests were not statistically projected to their respective populations, these results, together with the results of other audit procedures and tests, provide a reasonable basis for the assessment of DDC’s mitigation of delays and cost overruns of capital construction projects.

Endnotes

[1] Projects with total costs of at least $750 million.

[2] Prior to the establishment of DDC, City agencies were responsible for managing their own capital projects, which were not necessarily within their core responsibilities.

[3] Step streets are placed on hills that are too steep for roads. The steps provide pedestrians direct access in hilly areas.

[4] In its response to the Draft Report, DDC disagreed with the number of phases represented. For brevity, certain phases (e.g., planning and initiation) were combined in the audit report.

[5] In its response to the Draft Report, DDC argues that CPSDs are categorized by the agency as separate project investigations and are not considered part of the planning phase. Regardless of its categorization, CPSDs take place during a project’s initiation and planning phase and are represented as such in the report.

[6] DDC complied with a request from DSNY to compress the design schedule to one year instead of the standard two years.

[7] The lowest responsible and responsive bidder is a bidder who has the capability in all respects to perform in full the contract requirements and has the business integrity and reliability that will assure good faith performance and whose proposal complies with the solicitation requirement within the best overall value.

[8] Project start is when management of a project is officially transferred from the sponsor agency to DDC.

[9] The schedule revision history is maintained in Benchmark; however, the historical budget amounts are not maintained due to the absence of a history module in Benchmark. The budget information is transferred to Benchmark via an interface with FMS.

[10] A Gantt chart is a project management tool that uses a horizontal bar chart to visualize a project plan. It shows the start and end dates of project components, tasks, and resources. The chart helps project professionals monitor progress and understand the project schedule.

[11] The critical path method identifies the longest sequence of dependent tasks within a project. If those tasks are completed on time, the project can be completed at the earliest date possible. It also identifies the tasks that can be completed in parallel to critical tasks with buffer time around them. By prioritizing tasks that have no buffer time and completing other tasks around them, project managers can efficiently keep projects progressing to an on-time completion.

[12] https://www.nyc.gov/assets/home/downloads/pdf/press-releases/2023/CP_ReformTaskForce.pdf. Capital Process Reform Report.pdf.

[13] Listing provided in April of 2025 includes projects with Substantial Completion between FYs 2018-2034. Analysis of the April 2025 information is included in this report.

[14] The original Certificate to Proceed includes a contingency, which is generally 10% of construction cost. The budget at Substantial Completion includes the cost of DDC management of the project which is not reflected in the original Certificate to Proceed. The cost of DDC’s management of the project increases with project delays.

[15] Constructability analysis assesses whether a project can be completed and how to minimize the number of unexpected issues during construction that can lead to delays or increased expenses. Logistical constraints, material availability, site conditions, and the efficacy of construction methods all have to be taken into consideration.

[16] U drawings are drawings prepared by utilities detailing the location of underground infrastructure.

[17] Mechanical, Engineering, and Plumbing (MEP) drawings are contract drawing (blueprints) that detail Mechanical Electrical and Plumbing trades work on the project.

[18] Building Informational Modeling (BIM) is a digital collection of software applications designed to virtually construct the building on the computer. It is a 3D design visualization that facilitates the identification of conflicts (e.g., construction elements occupying the same physical space) early in the process.

[19] Review of NYC Capital Project Delivery (Report 1-2025), issued April 2024.

[20] Project Controls provides data analytics to help DDC adopt best practices in schedule, cost, and risk management. Project Controls provides project level support to individual projects (smaller projects) as well as programmatic and advisory support.

[21] In its response to the Draft Report, DDC argues that the target date established at initiation should not be used to measure overall timeliness because these dates are for planning purposes only. The audit used the target dates established at project initiation to measure the variance between the scheduled time when projects were initially approved with the time actually needed to complete projects.

[22] The data fields include project identifiers, including project ID, description, contract types, and sponsoring agency. Scheduling dates for each project phase, the planned amount, current phase, Benchmark status (active, canceled, completed, or on hold), and whether a project is an FEP project were also provided in the data.

[23] The overall delay is calculated as (Actual/Project Construction Completion date – Original Construction Completion Date) – (Actual Project Start – Original project start). This reflects the difference between the actual and baseline durations of the entire project.

[24] Phase delay is calculated as the difference between completion delay and start delay. For example, Design Phase Delay = (design completion delay) – (design start delay). This reflects the difference between the actual and baseline durations of the design phase.

[25] For the purposes of this audit, mid-range delays are those lasting anywhere from 90 to 720 days (approximately 2 years) while long-duration delays are those lasting more than 720 days.

[26] The information used for reporting is manually prepared by project management teams and then refined by the Project Controls unit for its intended purposes (i.e., Internal or External Reporting). For example, suspension of a design due to funding issues would not be counted as a delay in the MMR. Construction delays caused by private utilities also would not be considered delays in the MMR.

[27] In the MMR, DDC reports trends in completion rates. Table 1 shows delay data, which is the inverse of completion rates, calculated as 100% minus the reported completion rates.

[28] In its response to the Draft Report, DDC stated that a project listed as SANDRESM-1 was part of project SANDRESM1, despite it being issued a separate CP. However, the CP for SANDSRESM1, dated May 6, 2021, does not reference the CP for SANDRESM-1, dated January 10, 2020. Additionally, Benchmark listed these as two distinct projects. DDC does not provide evidence in its response to indicate that the $1.3 billion budget for SANDRESM1 includes the $106.9 million budget for SANDRESM-1. In the absence of sufficient evidence to the contrary, these are treated as separate projects in this report.

[29] Acronym definitions: DEP—Department of Environmental Protection; DSNY—Department of Sanitation; DOT—Department of Transportation; DSS—Department of Social Services; DPR—Department of Parks and Recreation; NYPD—New York Police Department; DOC—Department of Correction; MOR—Mayor’s Office of Resiliency.

[30] Auditors note that many of these projects are still ongoing, so the increase in cost when they are completed could be greater.

[31] In its response to the Draft Report, DDC argues that the RIKERSPMC project is not related to constructing borough-based jail facilities. However, the CP for this project (CP71292, dated November 29, 2021) clearly indicates that the funding is for project management “for the construction of new borough-based jail facilities.”

[32] This represents 9.1% of $9,562,240,121 ($870,163,851) plus $9,562,240,121 = $10,432,403,972, or $10.4 billion

[33] Performance against original budget was not reported in the preliminary FY2025 MMR issued in January 2025.

[34] The median cost variance is the middle value of a set of calculated cost variances, sorted by value.

[35] Fieldwork testing for this audit had effectively concluded by the time the MMR was issued.

[36] Libraries represent an aggregate of three separate entities—Brooklyn Public Libraries ($286,463,000); New York Public Libraries ($440,486,970); and Queens Public Libraries ($413,038,148).

[37] As of December 3, 2025, Benchmark shows the project as still ongoing.

[38] BIM is a digital collection of software applications designed to virtually construct the building on the computer. It is a 3D design visualization that facilitates the identification of conflicts (e.g., construction elements occupying the same physical space) early in the process.

[39] LOD describes the completeness of BIM elements. It is accumulative and should progress from LOD 100 through LOD 400. LOD 100 includes the building footprint used for conceptual design and phasing.  LOD 200 adds components such as walls and foundation for analysis of overall systems. LOD 300 generic components have been replaced with fully defined assemblies (gyspum wall board, Masonry – Brick, etc.). LOD 400 includes elements that are accurate in terms of size, shape, location, quantity, and orientation with complete fabrication assembly and detailing information. At LOD 400, clash detection sequencing and cost can be determined.

[40] Due to COVID shutdowns, and the resulting dramatic reduction in traffic, the $54 million budget for Traffic agents had a significant underrun, which combined with underruns in other unit price items provided funding for the project expansion.

[41] https://www.nyc.gov/site/operations/performance/capital-projects-dashboard.page

[42] Delay durations represent net delay durations. Delay represents the difference between the actual project duration and the planned duration.

