 Reports / Review of the Department of Sanitation’s Commercial Waste Zone Request for Proposals and Carter Selection Process 

 ARCHIVE from Comptroller Lander's Administration 

 Audit & InvestigationSanitation, Department Of

 Review of the Department of Sanitation’s Commercial Waste Zone Request for Proposals and Carter Selection Process

 September 17, 2025 
 | FP25-065S 

 Table of Contents

sections.RFP Complied with Local Law 199 and Soug:
According to the introduction to LL199, the intended purposes of the law were to:

• Establish new safety standards (improve training and safety standards to make industry safer for workers and the public);

• Reduce vehicle miles traveled (VMT);

• Reduce harmful emissions from waste hauling vehicles;

• Improve industry labor standards and uphold worker rights;

• Improve service for businesses (strengthen customer service standards and establish accountability); and

• Provide fair, transparent pricing with low prices for businesses

A review of the RFP found that it included elements intended to meet these purposes. (A full comparison of LL199’s intended purposes, with the requirements of the RFP, are found in Appendix V.) For example, regarding safety, DSNY required that carter trucks be equipped with a GPS telematics system to allow the agency to monitor and track critical safety information, such as hard stops, sudden accelerations, and speeding. (According to DSNY, its Bureau of Commercial Waste is responsible for monitoring this data.) Furthermore, DSNY required carters to submit safety records (e.g., inspection records), ensure that all vehicles have backup cameras by January 2026, and meet training requirements established by DSNY.

With regard to reducing VMT, DSNY required that the telematics system also allows the agency to monitor VMT in real time. For example, DSNY’s CWZ rules require dedicated routing per zone to ensure clear enforcement, route optimization, and performance tracking. According to DSNY, carter trucks are not permitted to operate across multiple zones in a single route unless explicitly authorized by DSNY. The telematics system allows DSNY to monitor each carter’s compliance with the requirement that they collect waste only within their authorized zone(s). Regarding the number of carters allowed to operate in a zone, the auditors found that the maximum number of carters (including the awarded carters and their proposed subcontractors) that could be operating in any given zone is nine, which is still significantly less than the 50+ carters that reportedly operated in certain areas prior to the CWZ program.[26]

DSNY required that proposals include a customer service plan, a plan describing practices to support waste reduction, a waste management plan, and a plan to reduce air pollution and greenhouse gas emissions from commercial waste vehicles. Regarding air pollution reduction, carters must have a plan to ensure that at least half of their fleet is composed of zero emission vehicles by 2030. In addition, DSNY required carters to submit the percentage of their fleets that meet EPA requirements for truck engines. The review of the proposals for each of the selected carters found that all 18 reported that 100% of their fleets complied with EPA requirements.

With regard to fair and transparent pricing, DSNY’s website has a Maximum Price Calculator, which allows businesses to view the maximum rate each carter is allowed to charge within their respective zone.[27] Businesses can use the information to negotiate lower prices with carters. Moreover, DSNY’s CWZ rules specify that customer service agreements between carter and business must include a clear description of applicable fees. However, as noted earlier, the auditors found significant differences in the maximum prices that could be charged by carters within certain zones, with no information in the RFP submissions explaining the reasons for these differences.

The auditors reviewed DSNY’s evaluation of the 34 proposals and found sufficient evidence supporting DSNY’s determinations that they were complete and met the requirements of the RFP.

According to DSNY, the information submitted by carters was reviewed in depth during the evaluation process. Regarding the financial documentation submitted by carters, DSNY worked with contracted vendor Arcadis to review and verify carters’ financial data. The verification process primarily relied on audited financial statements or tax returns submitted by the carters to ensure the authenticity of the information. Additionally, DSNY reviewed other requirements such as customer service plans, which included customer complaint procedures and language access plans. The Evaluation Committee used scorecards and notes to document the review of the detailed plans.

sections.Endnotes:
[1] DSNY conducts research and creates policies that govern when, where, and how businesses separate and set out their waste. Although it does not provide businesses with collection services, businesses must comply with DSNY rules and regulations.

[2] BIC regulates companies operating in the “trade waste” (or commercial waste) industry, including the approval, renewal, and denial of trade waste licenses. Businesses that remove their own waste can register with BIC as a self-hauler.

[3] DSNY garbage trucks are white. Garbage trucks of any other color are operated by private carters.

[4] New York City Department of Citywide Administrative Services. (December 20, 2021). City Unveils Roadmap to Prevent Injuries and Fatalities Caused by Commercial Garbage Trucks [Press release].

[5] LL199 defines “commercial waste” as all trade waste, except for construction and demolition debris; fill material; medical waste; electronic waste; textiles; yard waste collected by landscapers; waste collected by a one-time, on-call bulk waste removal service; grease; paper that is collected for the purpose of shredding or destruction; or waste that is collected by a micro-hauler.

[6] The initial term of any agreement is 10 years.

[7] LL199 states that DSNY can also consider any other information it deems appropriate regarding the evaluation of proposals.

[8] A “responsive proposal” is one that meets the material terms and conditions of the solicitation documents and all material requirements of the specifications.

[9] A BAFO is the final proposal submitted after negotiations are completed that contains the vendor’s most favorable terms for price and services or product to be delivered.

[10] DSNY has also referred to the “Technical Proposals” category as “Technical Plans” and “Technical Approach.” For this report, the term “Technical Proposals” is used.

[11] DSNY formed an Evaluation Committee (consisting of five members from DSNY and one member from BIC) to evaluate and score proposals received for RFP Part 2.

[12] For the purposes of this review, the terms “awarded” and “selected” may be used interchangeably to refer to carters that have been authorized to participate in the CWZ program. DSNY uses the term “awarded” to refer to participating carters.

[13] Local Law 145 of 2013, based on NYC Administrative Code Section 24-163.11, requires that every heavy-duty trade waste hauling vehicle that is owned or operated by a private carter be equipped with either (1) an EPA-certified 2007 (or later) engine, or (2) utilize “Best Available Retrofit Technology,” before January 1, 2020.

[14] The 60 “zone assignments” is based upon three awarded carters per zone multiplied by the 20 Commercial Waste Zones.

[15] In the context of government contracting, a responsibility determination is a formal assessment to ensure a prospective contractor can fulfill contractual obligations, including financial resources, performance schedule compliance, and a satisfactory record of integrity and performance.

[16] According to DSNY, subcontractors are proposed subcontractors until they are reviewed and approved (or disapproved) by DSNY ahead of each zone rollout.

[17] Recycle Track Systems NYC, LLC listed zero employees and zero trucks for its subcontractor Winter Bros. Waste Systems NYC LLC, a subsidiary of the Winter Bros. group of companies. At the time of the proposal submission, Winter Bros Waste Systems NYC, LLC was a newly formed entity that did not have operations in New York City. All 202 violations listed in the table occurred outside of NYC under the other Winter Bros companies.

[18] One of the 16 unawarded carters, Falso Carting, received a labor violation relating to wage theft.

[19] This system provides case information for New York State Civil Supreme Courts only. The auditors focused on the civil litigations filed between 2018 and 2022 within New York City.

[20] In addition, the CWZ program also has maximum pickup rates for containerized waste (includes waste in dumpsters, containers or compactors). Carters must provide discounted rates for recycling and organics collection compared to trash collection for curbside -pickup and containerized pickup.

[21] The “five-daymonthly rate” refers to the maximum monthly rate based upon a five-day per week pickup frequency.

[22] Businesses can decide whether to be charged by volume or weight and negotiate the frequency of pickups. Charges by weight are measured in pounds (lbs.) and charges by volume are measured in cubic yards.

[23] Los Angeles, CA, attempted to reform its commercial waste system in 2017, when all zones were implemented at the same time. According to DSNY, the abrupt implementation led to several unintended consequences, including skyrocketing costs for businesses, widespread dissatisfaction, and serious delays in trash collection. To avoid these issues, DSNY adopted a phased rollout plan to prioritize education, preparation, and stakeholder collaboration.

[24] New York Recycling only applied to zones in Manhattan; Royal Waste only applied to zones in Queens and the Bronx; Cogent only applied to zones in Brooklyn and Staten Island.

[25] Waste Connections and Royal Waste had overlapped in Queens West.

[26] The maximum number of nine assumes that each of the three awarded carters in the zone uses two subcontractors.

[27] DSNY’s Commercial Waste Zones: Maximum Price Calculator: https://www.nyc.gov/assets/dsny/forms/commercial-waste-zones-rate-calculator

[28] Each carter applied to specific zones and received a separate score for each zone. The overall score from the three scoring categories provided initial rankings for each zone and formed the basis for the preliminary assignments and rankings. Other considerations impacted zone awards and assignments such as a carter reaching the maximum number of zones that it had the capacity to serve. In those situations, the next applicable highest scoring carter was selected for the zone. Other considerations included a carter’s zone preferences and proximity to its garages and facilities near the zone.

